Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
> RjY wrote:
>> Patric Mueller posted:
>
>> Speaking of tedious repetitive actions:
>
> (Now, we're in the feature discussion; that's good.)
>
>>> [Wands of wishing are] 0 or 1 charges with 0 charges twice as often as
>>> 1. I didn't want to change the mechanism of wresting a wand just for
>>> /oW so I just reduced the average number of wishes you get from them.
>>
>> I don't know about this. At least in my vanilla games I've never
>> bothered with wresting charges because it requires entering the same
>> command into the game interface, over and over again, dozens, even
>> hundreds of times. Nobody has ever complained?
>
> In Nethack the wrest-last-wish is no problem, because you have a
> sufficient amount of wishes [from that wand] available. The wrest
> (mostly WRT but not restricted to wishing) is helpful in Vanilla for

Which wands (beside those that can't be charged) would you rather
wrest than charge?

> desperate cases where you've been stuck. It's acceptable for such
> corner cases to require tedious actions; after all you shall not
> be invited to use it as some standard means of problem solution.
> If in UnNethack the 0-charge-non-recharable-wand is the most likely
> object you will find, it's actually supporting the wishless conduct,
> and wishes [from that wand] only designed to help in those desperate
> cases. I want to conclude that even if I often inadvertently played
> wishless, I think it's not a nicely designed feature in UnNethack.
> Patric, if you think that there's too many wishes, just drop the
> "wrest-feature" and give that wand a single charge, or drop the wrest
> success probability to 10% (instead of less than 1%). I'd leave it

The wrest-feature has been with NetHack with the same probability
(why 1/11^2 ?) since it was Hack.

I don't know what the original author intended but if I look at the
current list of wands I have the impression that the wrest-feature is
only sensible in regards to the /oW.

It think it could be sensible to up the probability of the
wrest-feature. That would mean less time spend wresting a /oW and more
of the other wands turning to dust accidentally. :-D

> anyway as it's implemented in Vanilla; it's the most sensible, IMO.

You mean 4 guaranteed useful wishes from any /oW?

> (Mind, you've advanced to and cleared the Castle to get that wand.
> Is it that bad to wish for some items to reduce boredom or get

No, it isn't. That's the reason why I didn't axe wishes altogether.

There are just way too many wishing sources in vanilla.

> some helpful item that the stingy RNG refused to create randomly?

That's what the castle wand or a random /oW is for.

> Refrain from wishing for a magic whistle?

I've never had to wish for a magic whistle. They turn up rather often.

> Refrain from wishing for the missing seven candles?)

That's what magic lamp wishes are for. I've reverted the wishing
probability back to vanilla values. I wanted to do that when I
implemented non-magical wishes from magic lamps but I seem to have
forgotten doing it.

Bye
